WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.153
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.453 --> 00:00:08.410
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:17.631
Unit seventeen: Urban Inequality. In this unit, you will analyse inequality within cities and how to address it.

4
00:00:18.617 --> 00:00:20.268
spatial inequality.

5
00:00:20.968 --> 00:00:26.613
spatial inequality means unequal access to resources based on where people live.

6
00:00:26.913 --> 00:00:30.515
Spatial inequality separates rich and poor neighbourhoods.

7
00:00:31.215 --> 00:00:34.819
Your turn. Say: spatial inequality.

8
00:00:37.519 --> 00:00:39.170
spatial inequality.

9
00:00:40.183 --> 00:00:41.742
gated community.

10
00:00:42.442 --> 00:00:48.412
gated community means a residential area with controlled entrances, usually for the wealthy.

11
00:00:48.712 --> 00:00:52.244
New gated communities are appearing on the city's edge.

12
00:00:52.944 --> 00:00:56.270
Your turn. Say: gated community.

13
00:00:58.970 --> 00:01:00.319
gated community.

14
00:01:01.317 --> 00:01:02.596
urban poverty.

15
00:01:03.296 --> 00:01:08.338
urban poverty means poverty experienced by people living in cities.

16
00:01:08.638 --> 00:01:12.263
Urban poverty is often hidden behind modern skylines.

17
00:01:12.963 --> 00:01:15.998
Your turn. Say: urban poverty.

18
00:01:18.698 --> 00:01:19.943
urban poverty.

19
00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:21.916
eviction.

20
00:01:22.616 --> 00:01:25.672
eviction means forcing people to leave their homes.

21
00:01:25.972 --> 00:01:29.214
Families faced eviction when the land was sold.

22
00:01:29.914 --> 00:01:32.880
Your turn. Say: eviction.

23
00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:36.453
eviction.

24
00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:38.857
slum upgrading.

25
00:01:39.557 --> 00:01:43.902
slum upgrading means improving informal settlements instead of demolishing them.

26
00:01:44.202 --> 00:01:48.059
Slum upgrading brings water and sanitation to residents.

27
00:01:48.759 --> 00:01:52.317
Your turn. Say: slum upgrading.

28
00:01:55.017 --> 00:01:56.680
slum upgrading.

29
00:01:57.683 --> 00:01:58.928
right to the city.

30
00:01:59.628 --> 00:02:04.832
right to the city means the idea that all residents should shape and benefit from urban life.

31
00:02:05.132 --> 00:02:07.840
Activists campaign for the right to the city.

32
00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:12.179
Your turn. Say: right to the city.

33
00:02:14.879 --> 00:02:16.182
right to the city.

34
00:02:17.183 --> 00:02:19.253
housing affordability crisis.

35
00:02:19.953 --> 00:02:25.946
housing affordability crisis means a situation where homes are too expensive for most residents.

36
00:02:26.246 --> 00:02:30.080
The housing affordability crisis affects young workers most.

37
00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:34.930
Your turn. Say: housing affordability crisis.

38
00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:40.047
housing affordability crisis.

39
00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:42.515
urban displacement.

40
00:02:43.215 --> 00:02:46.829
urban displacement means people being pushed out of their neighbourhoods.

41
00:02:47.129 --> 00:02:50.638
Urban displacement often follows rising land prices.

42
00:02:51.338 --> 00:02:55.047
Your turn. Say: urban displacement.

43
00:02:57.747 --> 00:02:59.200
urban displacement.

44
00:03:00.217 --> 00:03:02.356
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:02.656 --> 00:03:07.514
Rithy, let's structure this like an essay. First, what is the core problem?

46
00:03:08.817 --> 00:03:15.379
To begin with, spatial inequality: services and opportunities are concentrated in a few areas.

47
00:03:16.683 --> 00:03:18.860
Good. And the second dimension?

48
00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:25.145
Turning to housing, the affordability crisis pushes low-income families to the edges.

49
00:03:26.450 --> 00:03:27.834
Which leads to?

50
00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:33.797
Urban displacement, and in some cases eviction without adequate compensation.

51
00:03:35.083 --> 00:03:36.862
What solutions would you weigh?

52
00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:46.956
On balance, slum upgrading seems more humane than demolition. In conclusion, the right to the city must include everyone.

53
00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:51.596
Grammar focus: Discourse organisation in essays.

54
00:03:51.896 --> 00:03:53.942
Essays need clear signposting.

55
00:03:54.242 --> 00:04:03.196
To begin with introduces the first point; turning to moves to a new topic; on balance evaluates; in conclusion closes.

56
00:04:03.496 --> 00:04:06.111
Vary your markers to keep writing fluent.

57
00:04:06.411 --> 00:04:13.461
Clear signposting helps readers follow long academic arguments; vary your markers to avoid repetition.

58
00:04:14.450 --> 00:04:17.265
Now read and listen. Two Cities in One.

59
00:04:17.565 --> 00:04:22.560
Many rapidly growing cities contain striking contrasts of wealth and poverty.

60
00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:30.261
To begin with, spatial inequality concentrates schools, hospitals and jobs in central districts.

61
00:04:31.550 --> 00:04:38.147
Turning to housing, rising land values have created an affordability crisis for ordinary workers.

62
00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:44.526
As a result, urban displacement pushes low-income families towards the periphery.

63
00:04:45.817 --> 00:04:52.681
Gated communities, meanwhile, allow wealthier residents to separate themselves from public problems.

64
00:04:53.983 --> 00:05:01.219
Slum upgrading, which improves informal settlements in place, has often proved more effective than eviction.

65
00:05:02.517 --> 00:05:08.173
The concept of the right to the city argues that all residents deserve a voice in urban development.

66
00:05:09.483 --> 00:05:16.266
On balance, inclusive planning appears essential if cities are to remain both prosperous and just.

67
00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:20.295
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:20.595 --> 00:05:23.187
Forcing people to leave their homes is blank.

69
00:05:23.487 --> 00:05:28.522
A, eviction. B, slum upgrading. C, gated community.

70
00:05:32.517 --> 00:05:34.574
The answer is A: eviction.

71
00:05:36.083 --> 00:05:38.779
Which marker introduces a new topic?

72
00:05:39.079 --> 00:05:43.290
A, Turning to. B, In conclusion. C, As a result.

73
00:05:47.283 --> 00:05:49.736
The answer is A: Turning to.

74
00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:53.714
Which marker shows final evaluation?

75
00:05:54.014 --> 00:05:57.969
A, To begin with. B, On balance. C, Turning to.

76
00:06:01.983 --> 00:06:04.366
The answer is B: On balance.

77
00:06:05.850 --> 00:06:09.765
Improving informal settlements instead of demolishing them is blank.

78
00:06:10.065 --> 00:06:16.610
A, slum upgrading. B, urban displacement. C, spatial inequality.

79
00:06:20.617 --> 00:06:23.789
The answer is A: slum upgrading.

80
00:06:25.283 --> 00:06:29.384
According to the text, what has often proved more effective than eviction?

81
00:06:29.684 --> 00:06:35.776
A, gated communities. B, slum upgrading. C, higher rents.

82
00:06:39.783 --> 00:06:42.619
The answer is B: slum upgrading.

83
00:06:44.117 --> 00:06:49.010
Now it's your turn. Part one: How can cities become fairer places to live?

84
00:06:54.310 --> 00:07:00.071
Part two: Organise your answer like a short essay with at least three discourse markers.

85
00:07:06.371 --> 00:07:23.097
To begin with, cities must improve services in poorer districts. Turning to housing, public rental homes could ease the affordability crisis. On balance, upgrading existing communities is fairer than moving them, so in conclusion, inclusion should guide planning.

86
00:07:23.397 --> 00:07:25.641
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:26.650 --> 00:07:32.321
Great work today! You learned eight new words and discourse organisation in essays.

88
00:07:32.621 --> 00:07:36.794
Today's value is community. Help the people in your town.

89
00:07:37.094 --> 00:07:39.990
See you in Unit eighteen. Goodbye!

90
00:07:40.290 --> 00:07:43.523
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

